#N canvas 20 20 2500 1300 12;
#X text 20 20 u_init -- owns the ordered startup. loadbang fires before ALSA connections exist \, so everything here is a timed sequence rather than a pile of del objects scattered through the patch., f 58;
#X text 620 20 creation arg 1: THE STATE DIRECTORY \, the same \$3 u_state and u_map take -- the recover breadcrumb is written there \, outside the patch folder. inlets: none. outlet 1: a bang when the boot sequence reaches the LAUNCHPAD stage \, wired to m_launchpad in u_root. A wire and not a send \, deliberately: the stage timings below are already coupled to u_tempo's 4000 \, and duplicating one of them into a second file is how that coupling gets broken silently., f 58;
#X text 20 160 Each stage reports to the disp bus as it is REACHED. Nothing here is acknowledged by any device \, so a stage name on screen means the sequence got that far -- not that the hardware answered. Boot sticking on one name still tells you where to look., f 58;
#X declare -path ../mac-stubs;
#X obj 620 300 loadbang;
#X obj 620 340 t b b;
#X obj 620 400 del 1500;
#X obj 620 450 t b b b;
#X obj 620 540 del 1500;
#X obj 620 600 t b b b;
#X obj 620 680 del 500;
#X msg 300 360 modal booting;
#X msg 300 470 modal wiring;
#X msg 300 620 modal launchpad;
#X msg 300 770 modal-off;
#X msg 600 810 status v0.3-ready;
#X obj 140 860 s disp;
#X obj 300 720 t b b b;
#X msg 900 490 sh wire.sh;
#X obj 900 530 shell;
#X obj 900 650 outlet;
#N canvas 40 40 1120 900 panic-display 0;
#X text 20 20 What is LEFT of the old safe-exit subpatch after Phase 6 lifted it. The Launchpad half -- returning the device to Live Mode -- went to m_launchpad \, which is the only file allowed to talk to it. The DISPLAY stayed \, because a panic is not the Launchpad's business and u_init owns the status line., f 58;
#X obj 20 200 r panic;
#X msg 20 330 status panic;
#X obj 20 385 s disp;
#X text 20 620 STICKY BY DESIGN \, AND SOMETHING ELSE MUST CLEAR IT. u_tempo bangs the stored BPM back out on every start and stop \, so the footer always describes the state you are actually in. Without that you could press aux \, watch the LED go green and the 404 start \, and still be told panic -- found on the device \, in Phase 5., f 58;
#X obj 20 260 t b b;
#X obj 300 330 t b b;
#X obj 300 440 del 1000;
#X msg 300 495 grid modal-off;
#X obj 300 550 s disp;
#X msg 560 440 grid modal 5;
#X obj 560 495 s disp;
#X text 560 620 ⛔ THE GRID GOES RED \, AND THE modal-off IS THE POINT OF THE del. g_grid's modal layer takes the whole surface and clears on modal-off OR on a 30 s safety TTL -- and leaning on that TTL would take the grid away for half a minute during the one moment you most need the instrument. That is item 251's failure in a different costume: panic used to hand the surface back and leave the grid dead until reload. ⚠️ THIS TOUCHES NO OWNERSHIP. g_grid owns the LEDs and disp is how a caller asks (C-5) \; m_launchpad owns the mode and is not addressed here at all \, so item 251 stays closed. 5 is the palette index for red -- the same 128-entry palette where 21 is green and 45 is blue \, confirmed by eye on the bench rather than assumed., f 58;
#X connect 1 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 2 0;
#X connect 5 1 6 0;
#X connect 6 1 10 0;
#X connect 10 0 11 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X restore 900 760 pd panic-display;
#X text 140 920 STAGE TIMINGS ARE GUESSES WITH EVIDENCE \, not measurements. loadbang fires before ALSA is up -- tools/README.md records init SysEx sent on loadbang going nowhere \, and item 266 on ref/module/boot.md is where the 1500 ms came from. If the Launchpad ever comes up unlit \, lengthen the second delay before suspecting the SysEx., f 58;
#X text 620 920 wire.sh runs from /tmp/patch \, which is Pd's working directory on the device. Every aconnect in it is allowed to fail \, so a missing device does not stop the rest -- and cannot stop the boot., f 46;
#X text 1080 920 shell is an external that exists only on the Organelle. mac-stubs/shell.pd stands in for it during the local syntax check \, and is never deployed., f 46;
#X obj 900 570 print wire;
#X text 140 1050 THE STAGES ARE MODALS \, so each one takes the whole screen at 16px while the meters have nothing to say. The last stage sends modal-off and drops v0.3-ready into the footer \, where a persistent status belongs. That also gives g_oled's modal layer a real caller in Phase 3 rather than shipping it untested., f 58;
#X text 620 1050 A message box typed modal booting ALREADY has modal as its selector \, so the list prepend / list trim chain this used to need is gone -- and with it one instance of the trap it was there to avoid. See C-6., f 46;
#X text 1080 1050 THE FOOTER IS HANDED OVER \, NOT SHARED. u_tempo drops the BPM into it at 4 s -- deliberately after v0.3-ready lands here at about 3.5 s \, so the boot status is superseded by something useful once the clock is up. CHANGE THESE STAGE TIMINGS AND THAT 4000 CHANGES WITH THEM., f 40;
#X obj 1180 240 loadbang;
#X msg 1180 375 0;
#X obj 1180 420 s midiInGate;
#X text 1550 240 SHUT OFF mother's OWN MIDI MAPPING \, FIRST THING. mother.pd runs [ctlin 21] to [ctlin 26] OMNI -- no channel argument -- and routes them to knob1 to knob4 and aux \, and it loads a new PATCH on any program change. The nanoKONTROL's top row is CC 21 to 29 \, so btn-t-5 pressed AUX and toggled the transport \, and btn-t-1 slammed knob 1 from 500 BPM to 10. Measured on the device \, not guessed., f 44;
#X text 1550 460 mother's own comment says it: all MIDI input can be suppressed by sending 0 to midiInGate. It gates only the MIDI-derived paths -- the front panel arrives by OSC and is untouched \, and our own ctlin objects read Pd's MIDI system directly \, so m_nano and m_organelle are unaffected. Cut It decodes every controller itself \; mother's defaults are pure interference here \, and the program-change one is a hazard on stage., f 44;
#X obj 1180 285 t b b;
#X obj 1320 330 del 2000;
#X text 1550 680 SENT TWICE \, AND THE SECOND ONE IS THE ONE THAT WORKS. The mother BINARY pushes its own midiInGate 1 over OSC about half a second after the patch loads \, which overwrote the value sent at loadbang -- measured with an [r midiInGate] print alongside \, which saw exactly one GATE 1 and no further pushes out to twelve seconds. The 2 s copy lands after it. The loadbang copy is kept because it costs nothing and closes the window in between., f 44;
#X obj 1350 420 s midiOutGate;
#X text 1550 900 AND THE SAME GOING OUT. mother echoes the Organelle's KEYS as MIDI notes and its KNOBS as CC 21 to 24 \, to every output port -- so playing the keyboard lit pads on the Launchpad \, and with the 404 attached instead it would TRIGGER PADS. The design says the keys go to the Volca and nowhere else \, so mother must not route them at all. Cut It will send them deliberately when the DIN interface arrives. This gates only mother's own noteout and ctlout \; our midiout is untouched \, which is why the clock still leaves on ports 1 and 3., f 50;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 1 10 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 2 11 0;
#X connect 6 1 17 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 8 2 12 0;
#X connect 8 1 19 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 16 0;
#X connect 16 1 13 0;
#X connect 16 0 14 0;
#X connect 10 0 15 0;
#X connect 11 0 15 0;
#X connect 12 0 15 0;
#X connect 13 0 15 0;
#X connect 14 0 15 0;
#X connect 17 0 18 0;
#X connect 18 0 24 0;
#X connect 29 0 30 0;
#X connect 28 0 33 0;
#X connect 33 1 29 0;
#X connect 33 0 34 0;
#X connect 34 0 29 0;
#X connect 29 0 36 0;
#X text 1180 620 THE LAUNCHPAD STAGE IS NOW AN OUTLET \, not a subpatch. Phase 6 moved Programmer Mode and the safe exit into m_launchpad \, and what stays here is the ORDER: this file still decides WHEN the Launchpad is initialised \, it just no longer knows HOW. The stage still reports modal launchpad to the screen at the same moment \, so a boot stuck on that name still means the same thing., f 44;
#X obj 1180 850 outlet;
#X text 2000 680 OUTLET 2 -- RESTORE THE SAVED STATE \, wired to u_state in u_root. It fires at the LAST stage \, about 3.5s in \, which is the earliest moment every m_ layer and u_map exist to receive what comes back. Earlier and a restore would publish into nothing., f 44;
#X text 2000 860 WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT A del INSIDE u_state. Startup ORDER is this file's job and the restore is a stage of it \, so putting a timer in u_state would mean two files owning the same sequence and neither one saying so -- exactly what the launchpad outlet above exists to avoid. It also fires FIRST of the three \, before modal-off and the footer \, so the screen settles after the state does rather than during it., f 44;
#X text 2000 1060 ⚠️ THE NEW OUTLET HAD TO GO TO THE RIGHT OF THE OLD ONE. Pd orders inlets and outlets by their x COORDINATE \, not by creation order -- so placing this box left of the launchpad outlet at x=900 would have silently made IT outlet 1 and sent the Launchpad init to u_state instead. Nothing would have errored., f 44;
#N canvas 40 40 1400 1250 recover 0;
#X text 20 20 PANIC'S SECOND TIER \, and the only recovery that exists for a device nothing can detect. u_present polls every device that can answer for itself and re-wires the rig when one goes missing -- but m_volca registers none \, because the Volca has no MIDI out at all \, so it can never be declared lost. It only comes back if a DETECTABLE device happened to fail beside it \, and on 2026-08-10 that failed exactly as the design allows: pulling the Volca's interface also knocked the SP-404 off the shared USB bus \, the 404 answered first \, the counter reset \, and the Volca sat unreachable until wire.sh was run by hand. Item 275. A reload re-enumerates everything. It is also the only thing that helps when the patch itself is wedged., f 62;
#X obj 20 300 r recover;
#X obj 20 350 t b b b b;
#X obj 900 350 realtime;
#X obj 900 300 loadbang;
#X obj 900 420 list prepend recover;
#X obj 900 530 text define \$0-crumb;
#X obj 900 475 text set \$0-crumb 0;
#X obj 520 420 symbol \$1;
#X msg 520 475 write -c \$1/cut-it-recover.txt;
#X obj 300 420 s panic;
#X obj 20 420 del 300;
#X msg 20 475 sh recover.sh;
#X obj 20 530 shell;
#X text 20 620 ⛔ THE TRIGGER READS RIGHT TO LEFT AND EVERY ONE OF THE FOUR IS ORDERED. The breadcrumb is stamped and written FIRST \, because after the reload there is no patch left to write anything. Then panic \, because killing Pd mid-note never sends the note-off -- the 404 would hold it \, and a panic that CREATES a stuck note is worse than the fault. Only then the del \, which gives the silence 300 ms to reach the wire before the process ends., f 62;
#X text 20 860 ⛔ IT FORKS ON AN EVENT \, WHICH BENDS PHASE 4'S RULE. That rule is one fork per load and NEVER per event \, written against error logging \, which cascades without limit. This cannot: it is rare \, user-initiated \, bounded at exactly one per gesture -- u_map's del 2000 is what bounds it -- and it ENDS THE PATCH. Nothing can cascade behind a command whose effect is to kill the process that ran it. Same terms as u_present's trailing fork., f 62;
#X text 900 620 THE STAMP IS ELAPSED MILLISECONDS SINCE LOAD \, not a frame number. The screen log's frame counter is u_mother-stub's and exists only on the Mac \; g_oled's is \$0-scoped and is nobody else's to read (C-1). What this needs is only enough to tell one attempt from another in a log \, and logroll.sh carries the previous session's console lines forward \, which is what correlates it., f 44;
#X text 900 860 ⚠️ shell's OUTLET IS DELIBERATELY UNCONNECTED \, exactly as u_state's is. There is nothing to read: oscsend is fire-and-forget UDP \, and the patch that would read a reply is about to be killed. That is the whole reason the breadcrumb exists -- see recover.sh and ref/module/map.md., f 44;
#X connect 4 0 3 0;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 3 3 1;
#X connect 3 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 7 0;
#X connect 2 2 8 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 6 0;
#X connect 2 1 10 0;
#X connect 2 0 11 0;
#X connect 11 0 12 0;
#X connect 12 0 13 0;
#X restore 2000 300 pd recover;
#X connect 16 2 39 0;
